{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787458175,"nonce":"qC6Ejcte6nP9XjlKFKdcIj9F9/8=","title":"`encrypt` in the Postgres secrets adapter passes no associated data, so a","message":"secret's `(ciphertext, value_nonce, wrapped_key, key_nonce)` tuple is portable\nbetween rows: anyone able to UPDATE the table can move a secret's value onto a\ndifferent `(scope, key)` without breaking any authentication tag.\n\nWhere — `crates/scarab-secrets-postgres/src/lib.rs:169-178`:\n\n    fn encrypt(key: \u0026[u8; 32], plaintext: \u0026[u8]) -\u003e Result\u003c(Vec\u003cu8\u003e, Vec\u003cu8\u003e), SecretError\u003e {\n        let cipher = Aes256Gcm::new_from_slice(key)...;\n        let nonce_bytes: [u8; 12] = random_bytes();\n        let nonce = Nonce::from_slice(\u0026nonce_bytes);\n        let ciphertext = cipher.encrypt(nonce, plaintext)...;\n\n`Aes256Gcm::encrypt(nonce, plaintext)` authenticates the ciphertext only.\nNothing binds the sealed bytes to the row identity they were sealed for. AES-GCM\nsupports exactly this via `encrypt(nonce, Payload { msg, aad })`.\n\nFailure scenario: an actor with write access to Postgres but no access to\n`SCARAB_MASTER_KEY` copies the four columns from\n`('env:production', 'DEPLOY_TOKEN')` onto `('repo:acme/app', 'GREETING')`. Both\nrows now decrypt cleanly — the master unwraps the data key, the data key opens\nthe ciphertext, every tag verifies. A pipeline that is authorised only for the\nrepo scope, and would be refused the production environment's secrets by\n`SecretScope::resolution_chain`, now reads the production token's plaintext.\nThe scope system is enforced entirely above the crypto, so the crypto does not\nnotice the scope changed.\n\nSame shape in reverse: the wrapped data key is also unbound, so data keys can be\nswapped between rows.\n\nThis does require database write access, which is a meaningful precondition — the\npoint is that envelope encryption at rest is specifically there to make DB\naccess insufficient, and unbound AEAD gives that away for free. The scope is\nalready available at both call sites, so binding it costs nothing.\n\nFix: pass the row identity as AAD on both seals — e.g.\n`aad = scope_text || 0x00 || key` for the value, and the same for the data-key\nwrap. Note this is a format change: existing rows have no AAD and will not\nverify, so it wants the `key_id` column from the sibling ticket to distinguish\nold rows from new, or a one-time re-encrypt.","files":null}]}